RPCS3 marks fmt::throw_exception's constructor [[noreturn]] and relies on
that to satisfy -Werror=return-type / -Werror=implicit-fallthrough after a
switch's default case (or a case end) just calls fmt::throw_exception(...).

The clang 22 toolchain built for REG-Linux no longer treats a [[noreturn]]
constructor as terminating control flow for these two diagnostics (it still
honours it for plain [[noreturn]] functions), so calls like
fmt::throw_exception("Unreachable") in PPUDisAsm.h, SPUThread.h, atomic.cpp
and bin_patch.cpp now trip -Werror. These are false positives: the
constructor never returns, so the "missing" return/break is unreachable.
Downgrade the two checks from hard errors to warnings under Clang rather
than patch every call site.

--- a/buildfiles/cmake/ConfigureCompiler.cmake
+++ b/buildfiles/cmake/ConfigureCompiler.cmake
@@ -47,10 +47,20 @@
 	add_compile_options(-Werror=old-style-cast)
 	add_compile_options(-Werror=sign-compare)
 	add_compile_options(-Werror=reorder)
-	add_compile_options(-Werror=return-type)
+	if(CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID STREQUAL "Clang")
+		# This clang no longer treats a [[noreturn]] constructor
+		# (fmt::throw_exception) as terminating control flow for these
+		# two diagnostics, so unreachable switch defaults/case ends that
+		# only call fmt::throw_exception(...) now trip -Werror as false
+		# positives. Downgrade to warnings for Clang only.
+		add_compile_options(-Wreturn-type)
+		add_compile_options(-Wimplicit-fallthrough)
+	else()
+		add_compile_options(-Werror=return-type)
+		add_compile_options(-Werror=implicit-fallthrough)
+	endif()
 	add_compile_options(-Werror=overloaded-virtual)
 	add_compile_options(-Werror=missing-noreturn)
-	add_compile_options(-Werror=implicit-fallthrough)
 	add_compile_options(-Wunused-parameter)
 	add_compile_options(-Wignored-qualifiers)
 	add_compile_options(-Wredundant-move)
